From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 13:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: arm64: record what lite translations embed, with
 relocation checks

A persistent translation cache (ANAL-381) has to give a translation from
one process the addresses of another. Lite translations embed guest PCs
(exits, the BL link value, ADR/ADRP, literal addresses), translation-table
slots, the main table, the region's hotness counter, helper and callback
addresses in this image, entry trampolines, and heap-built helper tables.
This names every one of them, without changing what is generated.

- MachineCode can record a PersistentReloc (offset, width, kind, value)
  per absolute operand. Recording is off by default: it costs a median
  2.4% of lite translation time, and nothing persists translations yet.
- x86_64::Assembler's Call, Jmp and Jcc with an inline 64-bit target
  record their target. MovqPersistent and MovqGuestAddr always emit the
  10-byte movabs and record the operand. code_gen_lib, the lite translator
  and the arm64 semantics player (the BL/BLR link value) use them. Above
  4 GiB, where Android puts guest code, Movq already picked that form, so
  the generated code is unchanged: Lichess's 183,699 region starts give the
  same 73,942,150 host bytes before and after.
- The URECPE/URSQRTE tables, built with new, get resource ids
  (GetLiteHostResource).
- runtime/arm64/relocatable_code: SymbolizeTranslation names each operand
  relative to the guest module, this image, the entry trampolines or a
  resource, and checks it against the installed bytes. RelinkTranslation
  gives a copy this process's addresses. FindUncoveredPointers looks for
  unrecorded addresses. Nothing calls these outside tests yet.

Tests (relocatable_code_test.cc), over fixtures that between them hit
every operand kind:
- a pointer scan of installed code, 8-byte windows and 4-byte windows
  below 4 GiB, against the image, entry trampolines, translation tables,
  resources, the guest module, installed code and counters;
- an in-process differential: a translation with the guest module above
  4 GiB, re-linked into one below 4 GiB with moved entry trampolines,
  equals that environment's own translation byte for byte;
- the same against a second process, where the image, heap resources,
  main table, entries, module and install buffer all differ;
- leaving out each record kind fails both checks; leaving displacements
  unnamed fails the differential;
- DISABLED_RegionCorpus runs this over an app's real region set;
  DigitalisBench.DISABLED_TranslateRegionCorpus times lite translation
  over it.

Co-authored-by: multica-agent <github@multica.ai>
